# The radius of bv_scale_2_entry: bv_scale_radius_2 on a guarded, the value that bv_scale_step_2 takes
# (rule R3, the factors of a fused operation). The radius only takes the magnitude of a and rounds
# its product, which needs no guard; it is guarded here so that the loop is bv_scale_2 on the
# guarded a, step and radius alike (proofs/theories/IeeeBv.v). One addition more per call, not per
# entry. Its claims are those of bv_scale_radius_2: the guard is the identity on numbers of the
# format.
kernel:        bv_scale_radius_entry_2
input:         stored m
input:         stored rx
input:         a
output:        rz
requires:      nonneg m
requires:      nonneg rx
ensures:       upper rz value=abs(a)*rx+m*unit(mul_2_1)/(1-unit(mul_2_1))*(1+u)+tiny(mul_2_1)/(1-unit(mul_2_1))
min_precision: 2
theorem:       proofs/theories/BvRadius.v bv_scale_radius_entry_2_correct
source:        bv_scale_radius_2 on the guarded a (rule R3, the factors of a fused operation)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
entry:         yes

ga = guard(a)
rz = bv_scale_radius_2(m, rx, ga)
